.TH GIT-WORKFLOW 1 2026-10-12 git-workflow "git-workflow manual"
.SH NAME
git-workflow \- git flow, GitHub flow or your own, with SemVer releases and guardrails
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git workflow
.I command
.RI [ args ]
.br
.B git workflow help
.RI [ command ]
.br
.B git workflow version
.SH DESCRIPTION
git-workflow makes a branching model the default path. The model is a
.IR flow ,
picked per repository (see
.BR FLOWS ).
.PP
In
.BR "git flow" ,
topic branches start from and go back into the integration branch
.RB ( next ),
release branches
.RI ( release/vX.Y.Z )
start from
.B next
and go into
.BR main ,
and hotfix branches
.RI ( hotfix/vX.Y.Z )
start from and go into
.BR main .
Every release is tagged on
.B main
and merged back into
.BR next .
.PP
In
.BR "GitHub flow" ,
everything starts from and goes back into
.BR main ,
and a release is a tag
.RB ( "git workflow branch pre" " and " release ).
Hotfixes start from the latest release.
.PP
.B git workflow branch finish
opens a pull request by default, and running it again once that's merged
tags and cleans up; nothing pushes to the main or dev branch. With a fork,
the main branch and the tags come from the
.B upstream
remote, and your branches go to your fork.
.PP
Release and hotfix versions are SemVer with a leading "v".
.B git workflow branch pre
tags a release branch as
.I vX.Y.Z\-rc.N
so a tag-triggered CI workflow can publish a prerelease before anything
reaches
.BR main .
.PP
Every command has a name, and the 1.x commands' short names work too:
.B git workflow b
is
.BR "git workflow branch" .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B branch \fR(\fBb\fR)
Start, prerelease, release, finish and delete branches following the flow;
options go to
.BR git\-branch (1).
See
.BR git\-workflow\-branch (1).
.TP
.B commit \fR(\fBc\fR)
Commit with the message as plain words.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-commit (1).
.TP
.B quick\-commit \fR(\fBqc\fR)
Commit, sync with the remote branch, and optionally push.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-quick\-commit (1).
.TP
.B push \fR(\fBps\fR)
Sync with the remote branch, then push.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-push (1).
.TP
.B sync
Fetch everything, then bring a branch up to date.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-sync (1).
.TP
.B pull \fR(\fBrb\-pull\fR)
Switch to a branch and bring it up to date with the remote.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-pull (1).
.TP
.B squash \fR(\fBsq\fR)
Interactively squash the current branch's own commits.
See
.BR git\-workflow\-squash (1).
.TP
.B aliases
Set up, list, reset or remove the alias sets. See
.BR git\-workflow\-aliases (1).
.TP
.B help \fR[\fIcommand\fR]
List the commands, with your alias for each, or open a command's manual page
(from a checkout, the page in its
.I man/
directory).
.TP
.B version
Show the installed version.
.SH ALIASES
git-workflow installs one program,
.BR git\-workflow ,
so its commands never take a name you already use.
.B git workflow aliases install
gives them short names again
.RB ( "git b" ,
.BR "git c" ,
\&...) by copying an alias file into
.I ~/.config/git\-workflow/aliases/
and including it from
.IR ~/.gitconfig .
That copy is yours to edit: rename, add or delete aliases, and messages and
.B git workflow help
use the names you choose.
.PP
.B git b \-\-help
opens this page, because git opens the page for the first word of an
alias. For a command's own page use
.B git workflow help branch
or
.BR "git workflow branch \-\-help" .
.SH FLOWS
A flow is a file of
.B workflow.*
settings (the format of
.BR git\-config (1)).
Two ship,
.B gitflow
and
.BR github ;
one in
.I ~/.config/git\-workflow/flows/<name>
is a flow too, and can define its own branch types.
.B workflow.flow
picks one. Unset, it's guessed: a
.B next
branch, or a
.B develop
branch (which then becomes the dev branch), means
.BR gitflow ;
anything else
.BR github .
.B git workflow branch config
shows the flow, why, and where each setting came from.
.PP
Settings are read in layers, a later one winning: the flow file; what the
repository looks like (its default branch, a develop branch); your global
git config; the repository's
.I .gitworkflow
file; the repository's own git config; and
.BR "git \-c" .
.PP
A repository's
.I .gitworkflow
is someone else's file, so it's read as data: values are checked against
the lists below and a bad one is reported and ignored, branch names must
be valid, includes aren't followed, and it can't set
.B workflow.remote
or
.BR workflow.upstream .
.SH CONFIGURATION
All settings are optional, under
.BR workflow. ,
globally or per repository, or in a
.I .gitworkflow
file. These apply to the whole flow:
.TP
.B workflow.flow
.BR gitflow ,
.BR github ,
or one of yours. Default: guessed (see
.BR FLOWS ).
.TP
.B workflow.mainBranch
Production branch; stable tags live here. Default: the default branch the
clone recorded for the remote
.RI ( refs/remotes/<remote>/HEAD ),
else
.BR main .
.TP
.B workflow.devBranch
Integration branch. Default: the flow's
.RB ( next
in
.BR gitflow ),
else the main branch. Leave it unset globally, so each repository's is
worked out from its branches.
.TP
.B workflow.integrate
.B pr
(default): finishing a branch pushes it and opens a pull request with the
forge, and finishing it again once it's merged tags it and cleans up.
.BR merge :
merge locally, and push everything in one atomic push.
.TP
.B workflow.releaseFromMain
.B true
lets
.B git workflow branch pre
and
.B git workflow branch release
tag the main branch directly. Set by the
.B github
flow.
.TP
.B workflow.remote
Where your branches go: your fork, if you work from one. Default:
.BR origin .
.TP
.B workflow.upstream
Where the main and dev branches and the tags live, and where pull
requests go. Default: a remote named
.B upstream
when there is one, else
.BR workflow.remote .
.TP
.B workflow.forge
What opens pull requests. Default:
.BR github ,
through
.BR gh (1).
.TP
.B workflow.prereleaseLabel
Default label for
.BR "git workflow branch pre" .
Default:
.BR rc .
.TP
.B workflow.strict
.B true
turns flow warnings into hard stops;
.B false
warns and asks. Default:
.BR false .
.TP
.B workflow.protected
Extra protected branches, in addition to the main and dev branches.
Multi-valued: use
.BR "git config \-\-add" .
.TP
.B workflow.versionFile
File that holds the project's version. When it exists,
.BR "git workflow branch pre" ,
.B release
and
.B finish
check that it matches the version being tagged ("v1.2.3" or "1.2.3").
Default:
.BR VERSION .
An empty value disables the check.
.PP
Each branch type has these, as
.BR workflow.<type>.<key> :
.TP
.B prefix
Branch name prefix
.RI ( release/ ).
One type has none; branches no other prefix matches belong to it.
.TP
.B from
.BR main ,
.B dev
or
.BR latest\-release :
what
.B start
branches from.
.TP
.B into
.B main
or
.BR dev :
what
.B finish
integrates into.
.B none
removes the type.
.TP
.B integrate
.B pr
or
.BR merge ,
overriding
.B workflow.integrate
for this type.
.TP
.B tag
.BR none ,
.B merge
or
.BR tip :
tag
.I vX.Y.Z
(named in the branch) on the merge, or on the branch's tested tip. Any
but
.B none
makes the type versioned: its branches are named
.IR vX.Y.Z .
.TP
.B backMerge
.B none
or
.BR dev :
after integrating into the main branch, merge it into the dev branch.
.TP
.B single
.B true
allows one branch of the type open at a time.
.PP
The 2.x names still work:
.B workflow.releasePrefix
is
.BR workflow.release.prefix ,
.B workflow.hotfixPrefix
is
.BR workflow.hotfix.prefix ,
and
.B workflow.finishTopic
is
.BR workflow.topic.integrate .
.SH GUARDRAILS
These warn and ask, or stop when
.B workflow.strict
is true:
.IP \(bu 2
committing or pushing directly on a protected branch
.IP \(bu 2
release or hotfix names that aren't vX.Y.Z, or aren't newer than the latest release
.IP \(bu 2
opening a second branch of a
.B single
type (git flow's releases) while one is open
.IP \(bu 2
cutting a prerelease from a branch that isn't a release or hotfix branch,
or with a tag whose version doesn't match the branch
.IP \(bu 2
tagging with uncommitted changes, with commits the remote branch doesn't
have, or with a version file that doesn't match
.IP \(bu 2
finishing a release with commits after its last prerelease, or releasing
with no prerelease
.PP
These always stop: reusing an existing tag, prereleasing a version that
already shipped, finishing or deleting a protected branch, force-pushing a
protected branch, pushing a branch that only takes pull requests, tagging
a branch that is behind its remote, tagging local commits the remote main
branch doesn't have, tagging the tip of a squash-merged release, deleting a
branch with unmerged commits without
.BR \-f ,
and finishing (or deleting the current branch) with uncommitted changes.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set, messages are not colored.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where your alias sets and flows go: its
.I git\-workflow/aliases/
and
.I git\-workflow/flows/
directories. Default:
.IR ~/.config .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/git\-workflow/aliases/commands
.TQ
.I ~/.config/git\-workflow/aliases/extras
Your alias sets, once installed:
.B b = workflow branch
and the other command aliases, and, if you install
.BR extras ,
general-purpose ones
.RB ( "git lg" ,
.BR "git s" ,
\&...).
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/git\-workflow/aliases/
The defaults they are copied from.
.TP
.I ~/.config/git\-workflow/flows/
Your flows; one named like a shipped flow replaces it.
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/git\-workflow/flows/
The flows that ship:
.B gitflow
and
.BR github .
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/git\-workflow/forges/
What opens pull requests:
.I github.sh
uses
.BR gh (1).
.TP
.I .gitworkflow
In a repository: its flow and settings, for everyone who works on it.
.TP
.I <prefix>/share/bash\-completion/completions/git\-workflow
Bash completion for
.B git workflow
and the aliases that run it. Git's own completion loads it the first time
it completes a
.B git workflow
command.
